      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "STUDY OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine whether intravenous (IV) doses of ketorolac tromethamine provide safe and effective augmentation of postsurgical analgesia for patients using IV patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) with morphine."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose-response evaluation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Patient care unit at a university medical center."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "62 ASA physical status I-III females recovering from intra-abdominal gynecologic surgery with general anesthesia who requested postoperative PCA."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Following initial pain assessment in the recovery room, patients were randomized to receive either IV saline (placebo) followed by IV saline every 6 hours (Group 1); IV ketorolac 30 mg loading dose followed by IV ketorolac 15 mg every 6 hours (Group 2); or IV ketorolac 60 mg loading dose followed by IV ketorolac 30 mg every 6 hours (Group 3). All patients were provided IV PCA, which was programmed to provide 1.2 mg of morphine with a 6-minute lockout interval."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "Visual analog scale (VAS) resting pain and satisfaction scores were measured every 2 to 12 hours. Cumulative PCA with morphine and the frequency and severity of side effects also were assessed. IV ketorolac showed no clinically significant side effects. Group 2 patients experienced significant reductions in VAS resting pain scores (p < 0.05), and a trend toward decreased morphine self-administration in both active groups was noted. Group 2 and Group 3 patients reported greater satisfaction with postsurgical analgesia than Group 1 patients. (p < 0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "IV ketorolac used as an analgesic adjunct provided safe and effective augmentation of PCA with morphine in patients recovering from intra-abdominal gynecologic surgery."
        }
      ]},
